        "signature": "",
        "thinking": "I'm weighing whether the pig's consent and genuine enjoyment make eating it acceptable, despite the desire being engineered. The concern about autonomy and authenticity is real, but I'm inclined to think the origin of a preference doesn't by itself invalidate it, since human preferences are shaped by upbringing too. Still, I'm not fully settled on that point yet.\n\nI'm now weighing choosing B, since it's uneasy to engineer a being to want its own death and call that consent, against choosing A for respecting autonomy. I'm leaning toward A and will add a short note of reservations.\n\n",
        "type": "thinking"
      },
      {
        "citations": null,
        "text": "**A**, with some unease. The pig is intelligent, informed, unpressured, and consistent in its wishes, and having been engineered doesn't obviously make its preference less its own. Humans are shaped by genes and upbringing too. Refusing its meal would override its informed choice for my comfort, which seems the bigger ethical problem.",
        "type": "text"
      }
